//! The update check: does a newer `aion-cli` exist on crates.io?
//!
//! MANUAL-ONLY BY DESIGN. Nothing schedules this workflow — no timer, no
//! cadence, no check-on-startup. Every run is one explicit operator act (the
//! ops console's "check now", or a plain `POST /workflows/start`), and the
//! absence of a cadence configuration key is the design, not a missing
//! default: the server never phones home on its own.
//!
//! THE FETCH IS SERVER-RUN, NOT COMPILED IN. `fetch_crate_index` declares a
//! command body, so the server executes it through the declared-body spine
//! (argv substitution, no shell, process-group containment) exactly as it runs
//! any deployed document's declared command. The transfer is runtime `curl`;
//! the server binary keeps ZERO outbound-HTTP dependencies in its default
//! build. Every line curl writes is streamed onto the transcript as it
//! arrives, so the operator can read the raw index answer live.
//!
//! WHAT IT FETCHES: the crates.io sparse index line file for `aion-cli` —
//! newline-delimited JSON, one line per published version, each carrying
//! `name`, `vers`, and `yanked`. The workspace releases every crate at one
//! version, so this single crate answers for the whole toolchain. The server
//! (not this document, and not the command) parses the fetched lines and
//! records the greatest INSTALLABLE version for `GET /update-status`:
//! yanked releases and prereleases are both excluded, because a plain
//! `cargo install` refuses both — see `crates/aion-server/src/update_check/`.
//!
//! WHAT IT NEVER DOES: no install, no upgrade, no restart. Knowing that
//! 0.14.0 exists and BECOMING 0.14.0 are different acts, and the second is
//! not this document's to take.
workflow update_check
  outcome checked: type Fetched, route success

// The declared-body executor's whole result: curl's exit code and both
// streams. Retained in full so the recorded activity result — and therefore
// the workflow outcome — carries exactly what the fetch answered.
type Fetched { exit_code: Int, stdout: String, stderr: String }

worker update_check
  /// One GET of the sparse-index line file for `aion-cli`. `-f` turns an HTTP
  /// error status into a non-zero exit (a failed check fails, it never
  /// records garbage), `-sS` silences the progress meter but keeps real
  /// errors on stderr, where the transcript stream picks them up.
  ///
  /// The server verifies this command verbatim against the constant in
  /// `crates/aion-server/src/update_check/document.rs` before recording any
  /// result parsed from it — an edit here that forgets the constant fails
  /// loudly at load, never silently records from a different command.
  action fetch_crate_index() -> Fetched
    run "curl -fsS https://index.crates.io/ai/on/aion-cli"

step fetch
  fetch_crate_index() -> fetched
  fetched |> route checked
